    "created_at": "2026-05-11T09:47:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-05-11T09:47:00Z",
    "body": "> > I guess we don't have to define (unordered) deep equals for v1, but I think the point around equals and equality still stands. And so we'd still have to define somewhere what equality means for platform objects since I don't think we want it to default to is.\r\n> \r\n> In what scenario wouldn't we want to default to 'is'? Looks to me like unless there is a special equality operator like in URLs or structs it's probably the natural fallback? Eg for js object references it's probably the most useful default.\r\n\r\nnevermind, I've looked at this again and was convinced. See new revision.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
