    "created_at": "2026-05-12T14:55:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-05-12T14:55:47Z",
    "body": "> I still miss a clear distinction between how you define equality for a concept and then how you compare it. Currently we do things like `<dfn for=url>equals</dfn>` but maybe it should actually be something like\r\n> \r\n> > A URL's equality, given a URL _a_ and a URL _b_, is:\r\n\r\nYes, we need to add that to the URL spec.  Thinking to do there what we do here for strings, where there is still a `<dfn for>` for compat but it is also defined in terms of the \"global\" equality.\r\n\r\n> \r\n> or maybe the `<dfn>` pattern is okay, but I don't see you employing that here for list and set (although you do for unordered).\r\n\r\n\r\nI think you missed this line, which accounts for both `list` and `set`:\r\n\r\nA [list](https://whatpr.org/infra/710.html#list) A is [equal](https://whatpr.org/infra/710.html#equal) to a [list](https://whatpr.org/infra/710.html#list) B if A has the same size as B and A[i] is [equal](https://whatpr.org/infra/710.html#equal) to B[i] for all [indices](https://whatpr.org/infra/710.html#list-get-the-indices) i of A.\r\n\r\n> Also for sets the unordered definition is the same as the ordered one, which seems wrong.\r\n\r\nRemoved the extra one.\r\n",
